#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71bf5bb6-53f4-52e6-9185-cb27120e2868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320428} {:answer/id #uuid "bd0cbf7d-8fc4-5457-a66a-8ba6e20f03fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320429} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ce7b9f-1b1f-5412-885b-5aec87e22bd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320430} {:answer/id #uuid "7239cb71-4f12-5950-b55e-47b880e374dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320431} {:answer/id #uuid "c07d7ce6-cac4-58c2-b80a-d72cadc7f4e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71bf5bb6-53f4-52e6-9185-cb27120e2868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320428} :answer-field/id #uuid "43bade0d-6102-5b59-b3a9-fc5f86ad2c80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "298e963a-ce31-5466-a327-a97e7b96e3d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-114033" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{3}{1+36{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $36{x}^{2}=(6x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=6x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{1+36{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{1+(6x)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7159a3d-ec8e-5f83-aa7f-107534ab4dee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{7}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320435} {:answer/id #uuid "02128603-73ff-5fe6-b741-2f7c9c4e81ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}\\,\\text{arcsec}(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320436} {:answer/id #uuid "282719bf-558a-5d37-8851-6bc4de23d2fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{arcsec}(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320437} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ca3b7e-6cbd-5c64-9a92-15c80432df71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{7}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320438} {:answer/id #uuid "2c8f6ca3-0450-51c8-937b-b837efe2a41d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(7x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7159a3d-ec8e-5f83-aa7f-107534ab4dee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{7}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320435} :answer-field/id #uuid "00edf96d-388d-510c-9abf-66bfe44e45fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "869c43e1-4bc9-51db-925b-6c1dc9459243" :question/math-academy-id "q-114055" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{7}{|x|\\sqrt{7{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{7}{|x|\\sqrt{7{x}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{7\\sqrt{7}}{|\\sqrt{7}x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{7}x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\sqrt{7}x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\sqrt{7}x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{7}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7}{|x|\\sqrt{7{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{7\\sqrt{7}}{|\\sqrt{7}x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{7}x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =7\\sqrt{7}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =7\\sqrt{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =7\\,\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =7\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{7}x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20fb36d6-035a-5391-ba4f-bd9e7475c99f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320442} {:answer/id #uuid "e5599341-a5a5-5cd4-8c05-af43c4f10e5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320443} {:answer/id #uuid "3e3bb541-797b-5901-a924-9d4a91ed3bb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320444} {:answer/id #uuid "fad3bede-bbb5-53bd-a0ea-f7b2e39a6ed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320445} {:answer/id #uuid "96c9b401-58a9-588e-b624-e330bfdeb3a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96c9b401-58a9-588e-b624-e330bfdeb3a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320446} :answer-field/id #uuid "460d7418-1ca7-5dc3-946f-fd68dee12a65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5e4c70d4-0535-5065-80e6-9431b5d6608c" :question/math-academy-id "q-114080" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/4)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Differentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (2\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/2} \\\\  & =8(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44f31708-7364-5b00-bc40-83773c796def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296163} {:answer/id #uuid "c8be7c0f-6c6c-5515-aa19-d61eb40f1685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296164} {:answer/id #uuid "9320b7ee-1d53-5394-885b-36f361f9eed1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296165} {:answer/id #uuid "0b740056-8d11-5c1e-837f-82dfab08f2f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296166} {:answer/id #uuid "a2122e1b-e597-5789-a69c-dbb150b6008f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b740056-8d11-5c1e-837f-82dfab08f2f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296166} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd9358d7-9edc-56fd-b26d-a923c3cd4f30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5a2898ff-e121-5eb2-975f-1ad5f1f98f96" :question/math-academy-id "q-114084" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/3)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/3}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/3}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c21e461-a671-5e4b-a962-f5a71b3900c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(7x)}{7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296205} {:answer/id #uuid "738cd36e-d0da-5231-93f2-6de34c018b34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(7x)}{49}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296206} {:answer/id #uuid "25a79dbd-654f-5b3a-915d-8352e289b51a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(49x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296207} {:answer/id #uuid "3558b5a2-0c0d-54d9-83b8-f17385e8fc2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(49x)}{7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296208} {:answer/id #uuid "e52250e6-c7fd-516d-9115-6e551b14a690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(7x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e52250e6-c7fd-516d-9115-6e551b14a690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(7x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296209} :answer-field/id #uuid "daf9c59a-5ecf-573b-9086-84dd83b422b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "975dd1af-9c7a-51cf-ad92-4355074f5c35" :question/math-academy-id "q-69475" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{7\\,\\text{d}x}{|7x|\\sqrt{(7x)^{2}-1}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{7\\,\\text{d}x}{|7x|\\sqrt{(7x)^{2}-1}}=7\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|7x|\\sqrt{(7x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $7x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=7x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=7\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{7}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}7\\int \\frac{1}{|7x|\\sqrt{(7x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x & =7\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{7}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(7x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
